For all $r>4$, there exists a  constant $C>0$ such that the following holds. For any positive integer $J$ and positive real numbers $t_0<t_1<\cdots < t_J$,  any $f_0,f_1\in L^8({\mathbb R}^3)$  and $f_2\in {L}^4(\mathbb{R}^3)$ with respective norms one, we have 
\begin{equation} \sum_{j=1}^{J} \|M_{t_j}(f_0,f_1,f_2) - M_{t_{j-1}}(f_0,f_1,f_2)\|_{{L}^2({\mathbb R}^3)}^r \leq C 
\end{equation}
where, with $e_0,e_1,e_2$ the standard unit vectors in ${\mathbb R}^3$, we have defined for almost every $x\in {\mathbb R}^3$:
\begin{equation}
M_{t}(f_0,f_1,f_2)(x) := \frac{1}{t}\int_0^t f_0(x+\tau e_0) f_1(x+\tau e_1) f_2(x+\tau e_2)\,d\tau.
\end{equation}

[Proved in Section 9]

 There is a constant $C$ such that the following holds. Let $J$ be a positive integer  and $(a_j)_{j=0}^J$, $(b_j)_{j=1}^J$  be increasing sequences of positive real numbers.    For $1\le j\le J$
 let $\phi_{j} :{\mathbb R}^2\to {\mathbb R}$ be a continuous function satisfying  
\begin{equation}
|\phi_{j}(u_1,u_2)| \leq (b_j)^{-2}(1+b_j^{-1}|(u_1, u_{2})|)^{-4} .  
\end{equation}
Let $K$ be a kernel such that
    \begin{equation}

    \widehat{K}(\xi,\eta,\tau) =    \sum_{j=1}^J \int_{{a_{j-1}}}^{{a_{j}}}  t^2(\xi+\eta)^2 g(t\xi) g(t\eta)  \,\frac{dt}{t} \,
    \widehat{\phi_{j}}(\tau,-\xi-\eta-\tau).
    \end{equation}

    Then estimate (2.11) holds for any tuple as in (2.12), (2.13).
